# Architecture boundaries

Full diagram and rationale: `ARCHITECTURE.md`. Summary, one-way only:

```
Domain <- Application <- Infrastructure
                       <- Wpf
```

- `PickMeWhatToListen.Domain` must never reference any other project.
- `PickMeWhatToListen.Application` must never reference `Infrastructure` or `Wpf`.
- Nothing outside `PickMeWhatToListen.Infrastructure` may reference
  `Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.*` — not even a `using` for a type name.
  If a ViewModel needs data, add/extend a method on `ArtistCatalogService`
  or a port interface in `Application/Abstractions`, and implement it in
  `Infrastructure`.
- `Wpf` is the only composition root. DI registration lives in
  `App.xaml.cs` (calls `services.AddInfrastructure()`) — don't scatter
  `new EfArtistRepository(...)`-style manual wiring elsewhere.
- No business logic in XAML code-behind (`*.xaml.cs`). Code-behind may only
  do view-local plumbing (`InitializeComponent`, wiring `DataContext`,
  `Loaded` handlers that call into the ViewModel). Everything else belongs
  in a ViewModel or an `Application` service.

These rules are meant to be enforced mechanically by
`tests/PickMeWhatToListen.ArchitectureTests` (NetArchTest) — if you touch a
layer boundary, update or add a rule there, don't just rely on this file.
